  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We adopt a multi-resolution optimization with coordinate-wise updates and curvature estimation to tackle the challenging non-convex Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Our method combines three key innovations:\n   (1) Standardized curvature estimation using automatic differentiation to guide gradient descent steps\n   (2) Multi-stage resolution scaling from coarse to fine\n   (3) Progressive Lag weighting based on correlation contribution to focus computations\n\n   This differs from previous approaches by leveraging analytical gradients rather than finite differences, and by adaptively targeting the most detrimental lags. Compared to different evolutionary strategies, this focused gradient approach is expected to converge more quickly in the high-dimensional space anchored by the integral constraint.\n\n   Important limitation: The method relies on gradient information which might poorly approximate the landscape in regions of high curvature.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Variables:\n   - h: 1D step function defined on [0,2] with samples\n   - dx = 2.0 / n\n   - Lag correlations: C(l) = dx * [integral of h(x)(1-h(x+l)) dx for shift l (estimable via correlation array)]\n   - Score: F(h) = max_l C(l)\n\n   Objective:\n   minimize F(h)\n   subject to:\n     0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n     sum(h) = n/2 (integral=1)\n\n   The optimizer uses projected gradients onto the feasible set (both box and integral constraints). At each iteration, we:\n     (1) Evaluate top 10 Lag candidates (dynamic selection)\n     (2) Compute gradient of F at those points\n     (3) Use curvature information (Hessian-vector products) to scale learning rates\n     (4) Update using coordinate-wise steps with gradient information\n\n   We maintain curvature history \u0393_{ik} for each coordinate i at each iteration k, updated by:\n      \u0393_{ik} = (I + \u03b2\u0393_{ik}) * (grad_i * grad_i^T)\n      Then use \u0393_{ik} inversely to scale gradient magnitude\n\n   The feasible projection algorithm:\n     - Clip to [0,1]\n     - Scale by target sum factor\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\n   def run_step(h, lag_subset=None):\n     if lag_subset is None:\n         Compute full correlation and select top 10 lags with highest score contribution\n         Also note negative lags above threshold\n     for each selected lag L:\n         Compute gradient g^L and Hessian-vector product (if curvature estimation is enabled)\n         Accumulate weighted gradient\n     Project to feasible space\n     Apply coordinate-wise updates using scaled gradients and curvature information\n\n   Main procedure:\n\n   Expandable pseudocode for clarity (although full implementation required):\n\n   def optimize_multi_scale(n_start=425):\n       # Phase 1: Coarse grid\n       h = initial_h_values\n       n_grid = n_start\n       do 100 evaluations at current grid\n       while time_remaining > 900s:\n           Update learning rate based on curvature\n           Run optimization on active coordinates\n           Save best candidate\n           Scale to finer grid if improvement > threshold\n       Refine with finer grid\n\n   Parameter configuration:\n     global_step_init = 0.1\n     local_step_init = 0.05\n     curvature_estimation_enabled = True  # Throughout all phases\n\n   Projection function (retain above)\n\n   Performs gradient optimization until:\n     (a) Time expiration\n     (b) Convergence (-grad^2 < 1e-12 or max_step < 1e-8)\n     (c) Max iterations\n\n   At termination, the best solution is projected and re-evaluated.\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n\n   Default configuration profile (for fine-tuning):\n\n   configurations = [\n        (n_grid=425, steps_per_grid=200, top_lag_count=10),\n        (n_grid=850, steps_per_grid=300, top_lag_count=15),\n        (n_grid=1700, steps_per_grid=500, top_lag_count=20),\n        (n_grid=2000, steps_per_community=800, top_lag_count=25)\n   ]\n\n   Trigger criteria:\n     \u2022 Increase resolution if continuous top Lag improvement > 0.95 over 25 iterations\n     \u2022 Monitor gradient dispersion (high curvature)\n     \u2022 Preserve budget proportion (80% across resolutions)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n   Phase-wise allocation (must be implemented):\n\n     \u25ab Coarse phase: ~15% budget\n     \u25ab Medium phase: 40% budget\n     \u25ab Fine phase: 45% budget\n     \u25ab Validation: 5% budget (finalize)\n\n   Time-critical operations:\n\n     - Monitor at halving intervals with early abort if:\n         remaining_time < 60s\n         stall_count > 5 (no improvement)\n         gradient norm > 1e6 likely diverging\n\n   Fallback mechanisms:\n     \u2022 50% of grid points use stochastic projection\n     \u2022 Exponential backoff for grid increases\n     \u2022 Gradient caching to reduce full correlation recomputations\n\n   Estimable dimensions:\n     Maximum grid size: 2000 points\n     Maximum iterations: 1000 steps\n     Acceptable memory: 3*(n_grid) floating point storage\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n   For final solution h:\n     (1) 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (clip verification)\n     (2) Is_close(sum(h) / 425, 1) with tolerance 1e-5\n     (3) Final score re-computation to ensure numerical contact\n\n   Statistical reporting:\n     \u2022 Minimal score found in primary grid\n     \u2022 Stability analysis (std of top 10 Lags)\n     \u2022 Runtime breakdown by resolution\n\n   Acceptable range for score: [0.36-0.39] to claim improvement beyond 0.3808\n\n   If time expires prematurely, the fallback solution from finest grid is returned.",
